{"about":"fab3fe93125ba81777325fc75a4b1873217880cd612e89c5f659b2b669bb771b","body":"The conjecture falsely claims that preferring a better-corroborated theory relies on an inductive assumption that the future will resemble the past. Popper explicitly argues that we prefer better-corroborated theories not because we expect them to be true or to succeed in the future, but because they have withstood the most severe attempts at falsification so far. The preference is a rational choice based on critical discussion and the current state of knowledge, not a prediction of future success derived from past performance.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"Mischaracterization of Popper's preference rationale"}